<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Escorial</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1563">1563</a>–<a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as San Lorenzo de El Escorial</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Escorial is a vast royal monastery and palace north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> between 1563 and 1584. Designed by Juan Bautista de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and Juan de Herrera, it combines a monastery, basilica, royal palace, library and burial place of the Spanish kings in a severe classical style. Its library holds important Arabic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, including the library of the Saadi sultan Zidan seized at sea in 1612.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-escorial.jpg" alt="El Escorial" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zvonimir Stamenov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monasterio_de_El_Escorial_en_Madrid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
